---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/seo/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ClosingCta from '../../components/seo/ClosingCta.astro';
import '../../components/seo/seo.css';
import '../../components/seo/ai.css';
import { pageViewer } from '../../site/viewer';
import {
  CONTENT_DATES,
  serializeJsonLd,
  organizationSchema,
} from '../../site/seo';
import { MCP, aiLaunchState } from '../../site/ai';
import { calculateQuote } from '../../server/pricing';
import { formatPrice } from '../../server/seo/offers';
import { BUNDLE_PERCENTS as bundle } from '../../server/seo/teammate-facts';
import quoteExample from '../../content/mcp-quote-example.json';
const coachingPrice = formatPrice(
  calculateQuote(
    { gameId: 'val', serviceId: 'vod', server: 'EU', units: 1 },
    { customerCurrency: 'EUR', eurToUsd: 1 },
  ).customer.amountMinor,
  'EUR',
);
const { viewer, csrf } = await pageViewer(Astro);
const title = 'Duoly MCP Server: Tools and Integration Guide | Duoly';
const description =
  'Connect to the Duoly MCP server for public games, services, quotes and availability. Read tool inputs, rate limits and booking link examples. No login needed.';
const request = JSON.stringify(
  {
    jsonrpc: '2.0',
    id: 2,
    method: 'tools/call',
    params: {
      name: 'get_quote',
      arguments: {
        game: 'val',
        service: 'duo2',
        rank: 'Platinum',
        server: 'EU',
        quantity: 1,
        currency: 'EUR',
      },
    },
  },
  null,
  2,
);
---

<SiteLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  indexing="index, follow"
  viewer={viewer}
  csrf={csrf}
>
  <article class="seo-page">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[
        { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
        { name: 'Duoly in AI assistants', path: '/ai' },
        { name: 'MCP developer guide', path: '/developers/mcp' },
      ]}
    />
    <header class="ai-doc-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Developer documentation</p>
      <h1>Connect your assistant to Duoly</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Use the Duoly MCP server to discover gaming teammates and coaching,
        calculate a quote and prepare a booking link. Catalog tools are public.
        Order status needs a private per-order code. All tools are read only.
        Payment stays on duoly.gg.
      </p>
      <p class="ai-release">
        {aiLaunchState().showSetup
          ? 'Available. Public tools are read only.'
          : 'Coming soon. Use this guide to prepare your integration.'}
      </p>
      <p class="byline">Updated 7 November 2026</p>
    </header>
    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="connection-h">
      <h2 id="connection-h">How do you connect to the Duoly MCP server?</h2>
      <p>
        The Duoly MCP server is a {MCP.transport} endpoint at{' '}
        <code>{MCP.endpoint}</code> that needs no account connection. Step 1 is
        choosing a protocol version from the {MCP.protocolVersions.length}{' '}
        supported revisions, step 2 is listing tools and step 3 is calling one
        with catalog tokens. For example, a catalog call from Duoly returns{' '}
        {coachingPrice} per hour for Valorant coaching. Coaching is excluded
        from bundle discounts. Teammate bookings receive {bundle[0]}% or{' '}
        {bundle[2]}% off at three or ten units. Order status is the one tool
        needing a private <code>dos_</code> code.
      </p>
      <dl class="ai-connection">
        <dt>Endpoint</dt>
        <dd>
          <code>{MCP.endpoint}</code>
        </dd>
        <dt>Transport</dt>
        <dd>{MCP.transport}</dd>
        <dt>Protocol versions</dt>
        <dd>{MCP.protocolVersions.join(', ')}</dd>
        <dt>Authentication</dt>
        <dd>
          No account connection. Order status requires a private dos_ code.
        </dd>
        <dt>Scope</dt>
        <dd>
          Public catalog, quotes, availability and booking links, plus limited
          order status with a customer-generated code. Social sessions are
          excluded.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Use POST for one JSON-RPC message with{' '}
        <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> and{' '}
        <code>Accept: application/json, text/event-stream</code>. JSON responses
        are returned. GET and DELETE return 405. Batches, subscriptions and
        streams are not supported.
      </p>
      <p>
        For 2025-11-25 and 2025-06-18, initialize your client, then use{' '}
        <code>tools/list</code> and <code>tools/call</code>. Send the negotiated{' '}
        <code>MCP-Protocol-Version</code> header. The transport is stateless
        with no session ID. For Claude attribution, use
        <code>https://duoly.gg/mcp?client=claude</code>. The client query hint
        also accepts chatgpt or gemini. It affects only the agent tag on booking
        links, never identity, pricing or permissions.
      </p>
      <p>
        For 2026-07-28, use <code>server/discover</code> without initialization.
        Each request needs <code>MCP-Protocol-Version: 2026-07-28</code>,{' '}
        <code>Mcp-Method</code> matching the method, and <code>Mcp-Name</code>{' '}
        matching the tool for tool calls. Include protocol version, client info
        and client capabilities in <code>params._meta</code>. Use an SDK
        matching the protocol revision.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="tools-h">
      <h2 id="tools-h">Which Duoly MCP tool should a client call first?</h2>
      <p>
        find_session is the tool to call first, because a goal plus a game
        returns up to three priced services with booking links in one round
        trip. Step 1 is find_session, step 2 is get_quote for one exact
        selection, and step 3 is create_booking_link. For example, a Valorant
        coaching search returns the {coachingPrice} per hour VOD Review. A ten
        game teammate quote arrives with the {bundle[2]}% bundle discount
        applied. list_games and list_services exist for browsing, while
        get_availability reports public online counts without reserving anybody.
        Goal tokens are duo, coaching, ranked, casual and teams, with training
        and other accepted as category aliases. Finally, each result has one
        readable summary and full schema-validated structuredContent, without
        JSON duplicated into text. Card-only data stays in result metadata.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="booklink-h">
      <h2 id="booklink-h">What does a Duoly booking link carry?</h2>
      <p>
        A Duoly booking link is a prefilled selection on duoly.gg rather than an
        order. Share bookUrl as the primary link so a person can book. The link
        preserves supplied rank, region and quantity, plus role, coaching
        character, voice, mood and teammate count. Minecraft links also preserve
        server or world and edition. For example, a {coachingPrice} per hour
        Valorant coaching link from Duoly opens the checkout with the service
        already chosen.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three habits keep a client honest. First, use exact game, rank and
        region tokens from the catalog, and use list_services for exact role and
        seat options. Second, remember an availability signal or quote creates
        no order, so use get_quote only for a selected service and
        create_booking_link only to produce a final link. Third, leave private
        notes, IGN and payment choices on Duoly, where people confirm remaining
        options. Finally, rank-priced services show a from price and a typical
        one-unit price; exact totals include bundle savings of {bundle[0]}%,{' '}
        {bundle[1]}% or {bundle[2]}%.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="table-h">
      <h2 id="table-h">What are the inputs and outputs of each tool?</h2>
      <p>
        Each Duoly tool takes catalog tokens and returns structured data plus a
        booking URL. First, list_games needs nothing, while list_services and
        create_booking_link need a game. Second, get_quote needs a game, a
        service, a server, a quantity and a currency, plus a rank when the
        service is rank priced. Coaching returns {coachingPrice} per hour;
        teammate bookings receive {bundle[2]}% off a ten game total. Third,
        find_session takes the same selection loosely, filling gaps with a
        labelled from price and a missingInputs list. Finally, get_order_status
        needs only the private <code>dos_</code> code, and returns acceptance,
        teammate display names and a next step. For example, a find_session call
        for a Valorant duo under five euros returns services, prices and a
        bookUrl in one structured result, ready to render using Claude cards.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="reference-h">
      <h2 id="reference-h">Which inputs does each Duoly MCP tool accept?</h2>
      <p>
        The v1 tool reference below is the authoritative input and output list,
        matching the live schema. For example, get_quote returns {coachingPrice}{' '}
        per hour for Valorant coaching from Duoly, without a quantity discount.
        A ten game teammate quote includes {bundle[2]}% off. On narrow screens,
        scroll the table.
      </p>
      <div
        class="ai-table-wrap"
        role="region"
        aria-label="MCP tool inputs and outputs"
        tabindex="0"
      >
        <table class="ai-table">
          <caption>v1 tool reference</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Tool</th>
              <th scope="col">Inputs</th>
              <th scope="col">Output</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {MCP.tools.map((tool) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  <code>{tool.name}</code>
                </th>
                <td>{tool.inputs}</td>
                <td>{tool.outputs}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="accuracy-h">
      <h2 id="accuracy-h">How accurate are availability and quote results?</h2>
      <p>
        An availability result is a cached public signal rather than a
        reservation, and a quote is a calculation rather than a held price.
        First, treat an online count as an upper bound, because a listed
        teammate may already sit in a session. Second, request a fresh quote
        whenever a game, service, rank, quantity or currency changes. Third,
        remember that bundle discounts of {bundle[0]}%, {bundle[1]}% and{' '}
        {bundle[2]}% land in the total rather than in a from price. For example,
        a single Valorant coaching hour reads {coachingPrice} from Duoly while a
        ten game duo total already includes {bundle[2]}% off.
      </p>
      <p>
        Availability uses a 30-second cached aggregate. Online counts can
        include teammates already in a session. Counts are shown only when
        positive. Zero and unavailable counts are omitted, as are overall
        totals. Any historical match median covers sitewide accepted orders from
        the last 30 days, including services outside this catalog. It does not
        predict a wait time for a game.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="quoteinputs-h">
      <h2 id="quoteinputs-h">Which inputs change a Duoly quote?</h2>
      <p>
        A Duoly quote is a calculation over five inputs: game, service, rank,
        quantity and currency. First, the game and service decide the base rate,
        so a Valorant coaching hour from Duoly reads {coachingPrice} at any
        quantity. Second, rank moves the rate on rank-priced teammate services,
        which is why a from price and a typical one-unit price are both
        returned. Third, quantity applies the bundle tiers, dropping a three
        game total by {bundle[0]}%, a five game total by {bundle[1]}% and a ten
        game total by {bundle[2]}%. For example, a ten game duo booking is
        cheaper per game than a single booking of the same service. Finally,
        request a fresh quote whenever one of the five inputs changes, because
        checkout recalculates the final figure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Amounts depend on the game, service, rank, quantity and currency. Get a
        fresh quote when those choices change. Checkout calculates the final
        price, including any applicable account eligibility. USD quotes are
        estimates.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="tokens-h">
      <h2 id="tokens-h">Which service and rank tokens are valid?</h2>
      <p>
        A valid token is a string list_services returned for that game, never a
        display name typed by hand. First, use the exact rank and region tokens
        from list_services, and omit rank entirely for flat-priced services.
        Second, services appear only when the pricing code accepts them, so a
        missing service is a catalog answer rather than a bug. For example,
        Valorant coaching is flat at {coachingPrice} per hour from Duoly, so a
        rank token on that service is rejected. Minecraft requires a server or
        world string and a Java or Bedrock edition. Services appear only when
        the pricing code accepts them. Dynamic streamer bookings are excluded.
        list_services includes catalog descriptions and a calculated from price
        in EUR for one game or hour. find_session returns up to three matching
        services, a total price for the requested quantity and missingInputs.
        Incomplete selections have labelled from prices; unspecified quantity
        and currency use one unit and EUR for that preview. A budget is a total
        cap and needs a currency before it is applied. Goal tokens are duo,
        coaching, ranked, casual and teams; training and other are category
        aliases.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="quote-h">
      <h2 id="quote-h">How do you request a quote over JSON-RPC?</h2>
      <p>
        A quote request is a single <code>tools/call</code> message naming
        get_quote and a complete selection. The example below uses protocol
        2025-11-25 after initialization, so discover valid service and rank
        values with <code>list_services</code> first. Step 1 is listing services
        for the game, step 2 is picking a service and rank token, and step 3 is
        the call itself. For example, the Platinum Valorant Duo Pro request
        below returns a formatted price, an amount in minor units and a booking
        URL from Duoly, while a Valorant coaching hour would return{' '}
        {coachingPrice} with no rank at all.
      </p>
      <p class="ai-code-label">Request</p>
      <pre class="ai-code"><code>{request}</code></pre>
      <p class="ai-code-label">Response</p>
      <p>
        A successful call returns MCP content and structured quote data. We
        captured the example below from the local tool implementation on 7
        November 2026 using the real pricing calculator and FX loader, so the
        shape matches production even though the amount changes. For example, a
        Valorant coaching hour reads {coachingPrice} from Duoly today. Finally,
        request a fresh quote before booking rather than caching the figure.
      </p>
      <pre class="ai-code"><code>{JSON.stringify(quoteExample, null, 2)}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Handle tool errors before showing a price or link. Do not treat an error
        message, stale quote or empty availability result as a confirmed
        session.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="inspector-h">
      <h2 id="inspector-h">How do you test a Duoly integration locally?</h2>
      <p>
        MCP Inspector is the fastest way to test a Duoly integration without
        writing a client. Step 1 starts the Inspector locally and opens the
        printed URL, step 2 connects using {MCP.transport} with the endpoint
        above and no authentication, and step 3 initializes, lists tools and
        tries a catalog request before a quote. For example, a list_services
        call for Valorant returns coaching at {coachingPrice} per hour from
        Duoly, which confirms both the transport and the pricing path in a
        single request, and a ten game teammate quote confirms the {bundle[2]}%
        bundle path. Additionally, an unexpected 405 means a GET or DELETE
        reached the endpoint, since only POST carries a JSON-RPC message.
        Finally, the command line form below prints the tool list without a
        browser.
      </p>
      <pre class="ai-code"><code>npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector</code></pre>
      <p>For a command-line discovery check:</p>
      <pre class="ai-code"><code>{`npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector --cli ${MCP.endpoint} --transport http --method tools/list`}</code></pre>
    </section>
    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
      <h2 id="limits-h">What are the Duoly MCP rate limits?</h2>
      <p>
        A Duoly rate limit is a per-IP budget measured in accepted POST
        requests, including discovery and initialization. First, respect both
        the per minute and the per day caps listed below, because every accepted
        request counts against them. Second, back off on HTTP 429 using the
        supplied Retry-After rather than retrying in a tight loop or rotating
        addresses. Third, keep request bodies small, because an oversized body
        is rejected before pricing ever runs. For example, pricing Valorant
        coaching at {coachingPrice} per hour from Duoly and then a ten game
        teammate total at {bundle[2]}% off costs only two calls, which leaves
        plenty of headroom for a long conversation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Limits are {MCP.rateLimits.minute} requests per minute and{' '}
        {MCP.rateLimits.day.toLocaleString('en-US')} per day per IP. On HTTP
        429, respect the response’s retry guidance and back off. Do not retry in
        a tight loop or rotate addresses to bypass the limit. Each accepted POST
        counts, including discovery and initialization. Request bodies are
        limited to 16,384 bytes; the complete tool response is capped at 65,536
        bytes. HTTP 429 includes Retry-After and error.data.retryAfterSeconds.
        HTTP 503 means required server configuration is unavailable.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="origins-h">
      <h2 id="origins-h">Which origins may call the Duoly endpoint?</h2>
      <p>
        An accepted origin is any HTTPS origin, plus native and server clients
        that send none at all. First, assistants such as Claude and ChatGPT can
        connect directly without a proxy in between. Second, malformed, opaque
        and plain HTTP origins are rejected before a tool runs. Third, no
        cookies or credentials are ever accepted, because the catalog needs no
        identity to return {coachingPrice} per hour for Valorant coaching from
        Duoly or a {bundle[2]}% bundle total. Finally, the optional client query
        hint accepts claude, chatgpt or gemini, and affects only the agent tag
        on booking links rather than identity, pricing or permissions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Any HTTPS origin may call the endpoint, so assistants such as Claude and
        ChatGPT can connect directly. Native and server clients may omit Origin.
        Malformed, opaque and plain HTTP origins are rejected. No cookies or
        credentials are accepted.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="privacy-rules-h">
      <h2 id="privacy-rules-h">What data should a Duoly client send?</h2>
      <p>
        A Duoly request is a set of catalog choices and nothing else. First,
        send only the structured values a tool needs, for example a game, a
        service, a rank and a quantity. Second, keep every identifier out of the
        request, because catalog tools read no accounts at all. Finally, prices
        need no identity, which is why a {coachingPrice} per hour quote from
        Duoly arrives without a login. Teammate quotes also include any
        applicable {bundle[0]}% or {bundle[2]}% quantity discount without a
        login.
      </p>
      <p>
        Send only the structured catalog choices needed by a tool. Never send
        names, emails, chat history, passwords, card details or account tokens.
        Catalog tools do not read accounts. get_order_status reads only the
        limited order projection authorized by its code. Never log or persist
        that code. Abuse prevention uses an IP-derived identifier; hosting
        infrastructure also processes connection metadata.
      </p>
      <p>
        These tools cannot charge, reserve or cancel. A private order code
        allows only a limited read of that one order. Never promise a specific
        start time, teammate rank or in-game result. Link users to Duoly to
        review and complete their own booking. See our{' '}
        <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> and <a href="/terms">terms</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
    <ClosingCta
      heading="Building a Duoly integration?"
      text="A Duoly integration is a read-only MCP client that ends on duoly.gg. Contact the team for partner questions and compatibility checks before shipping to users."
      extra={`Three rules keep an integration correct. First, never present a quote as a reservation, because a teammate accepts each order from the Duoly queue rather than receiving an assignment. Second, always surface bookUrl as the primary link, so a person reviews the final price before paying on duoly.gg. Third, send only catalog tokens, never names, emails or payment details. For example, a Valorant coaching hour reads ${coachingPrice} from Duoly without bundle discounts. Teammate bookings receive ${bundle[0]}%, ${bundle[1]}% and ${bundle[2]}% off at three, five and ten units. Handle HTTP 429 with the supplied Retry-After rather than retrying in a tight loop.`}
      href="/contact"
      label="Contact Duoly"
    />
    <nav class="related" aria-label="Related pages">
      <h2>For your users</h2>
      <div class="chips">
        <a href="/ai">Using Duoly in AI assistants</a>
        <a href="/games">Games and coaching catalog</a>
      </div>
    </nav>
  </article>
  <script
    slot="head"
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={serializeJsonLd({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'TechArticle',
      headline: 'Connect your assistant to Duoly',
      description,
      url: new URL('/developers/mcp', Astro.site!).href,
      datePublished: '2026-11-06',
      dateModified: CONTENT_DATES['/developers/mcp'],
      author: organizationSchema(Astro.site!),
      about: 'Public read-only Duoly MCP integration',
    })}
  />
</SiteLayout>
